   "source": [
    "# BT4222 GROUP 7: Twitter Bot Fraud Detection"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "305943fd",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "## Initialising Edges, tweets, users metadata Files "
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "b0d56054",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "### Importing Libaries"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": 1,
   "id": "4308e4d9",
   "metadata": {},
   "outputs": [],
   "source": [
    "import pandas as pd\n",
    "import numpy as np\n",
    "import matplotlib.pyplot as plt"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "87f5db07",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "## Final Dataset Before Graph Conversion\n",
    "\n",
    "This section prepares the final user–user interaction dataset before converting it into a graph learning format.\n",
    "\n",
    "The raw data contains multiple entity types, including users, tweets, lists, and hashtags. To make the data suitable for graph learning, this section transforms the original edge table into a unified multi-relational user–user edge list, where each directed edge represents a specific interaction between two users. These interactions include follow, like, mention, reply, retweet, quote, community-based relations, and usage of the same  hasthtags.\n",
    "\n",
    "Rather than using the raw heterogeneous edge table directly, this implementation first resolves intermediate entities such as tweets and lists so that all final edges are expressed consistently as **user → relation → user**.\n",
    "\n",
    "Pipeline:\n",
    "1. Read and load the raw edge table, user metadata, list data, and hashtag data.\n",
    "2. Standardise identifiers and split the raw edges by relation type.\n",
    "3. Build a `tweet_to_user` mapping so tweet-level interactions can be traced back to the corresponding author.\n",
    "4. Convert each interaction type into a user–user relation, including follows, likes, mentions, replies, retweets, quotes, community-based links and usage of the same hashtag.\n",
    "5. Combine all relation-specific edge tables into one final `df_user_user_edges_all` dataset and filter the final edge list to retain only edges where atleast one endpoints belong to the 20k labelled user set.\n",
    "6. Produce summary statistics and connectivity checks to validate the final dataset before graph conversion."
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "1d8a4420",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "### Reading Files"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": 2,
   "id": "64b5d902",
   "metadata": {},
   "outputs": [],
   "source": [
    "pd.set_option(\"display.max_columns\", 200)\n",
    "pd.set_option(\"display.max_rows\", 100)\n",
    "pd.set_option(\"display.width\", 200)\n",
    "\n",
    "df_edges = pd.read_parquet(\"datasets/df_edges_final.parquet\")\n",
    "df_user = pd.read_parquet(\"datasets/df_user_meta_full.parquet\")\n",
    "df_list = pd.read_csv(\"datasets/list.csv\")\n",
    "df_hashtag = pd.read_json(\"datasets/hashtag.json\")"
   ]
  },
